From f5bdef704a36dd92bdef01869763657c74d8811b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 00:33:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: iris: ROG5: review fixes for 0157-0161 (PM vs IRQ,
 close ownership, unwind, remove)

Review of 0157-0161 (GPT-6.1-Sol, read-only) found:

- Runtime PM against the interrupt thread: the PM callbacks synchronised
  the IRQ, but the thread can wait for inst->lock held by a task that waits
  for that same PM transition (pm_runtime_resume_and_get() on a command),
  or call it itself. The PM callbacks no longer wait for the thread;
  iris_vpu_power_off() masks the line with disable_irq_nosync() (its
  callers hold core->lock), and the thread already touches registers only
  while core->powered, checked under core->lock. The explicit teardown
  paths (work item, deinit, init unwind) still synchronise the IRQ outside
  core->lock while holding a runtime PM reference.
- Close freed firmware-visible memory before the firmware let go of it:
  after a fatal error the containment work is flushed before vb2 releases
  the client buffers, and the internal buffers and the EOS buffer are only
  freed when the firmware answered SESSION_END (any answer) or was shut
  down by the containment; otherwise they are leaked on purpose.
- The HFI ops were set after firmware boot, while power on had already
  enabled the IRQ: set them before power on; the thread checks them.
- The init-failure unwind ignored the PAS shutdown result: it now keeps
  clocks, votes and memory (fw_held) like iris_core_teardown(), and leaves
  a core the containment already took over alone.
- Removal: containment work is scheduled under core->lock and not at all
  once iris_remove() set core->removing, so the final cancel is reliable.
- Nothing is sent after SESSION_END (the session command path refuses it,
  and the expected-answer bookkeeping is left to END), so a second
  streamoff cannot steal END's acknowledgement.
- The HFI debug config packet is 28 bytes like stock (trailing word).

Second review round:
- Close keeps the client buffers and the m2m context too (leaked, logged)
  when the containment could not shut the firmware down (fw_held).
- A content-protection failure after a successful authentication shuts
  the image down and, if TrustZone refuses that, records fw_held and
  fatal, so the init unwind keeps power and memory as well.
- iris_remove() disables the line after deinit (a held core keeps it
  enabled) before the lock is destroyed.
- gen2's system error goes through iris_core_fatal() too (latched,
  honours removal; the work no longer reloads for either generation).

Also: the HFI trace keeps 14 words per packet, so an EMPTY_BUFFER's
flags, lengths and buffer address and an event's data words are visible.

Not changed: gen2 response handling still walks the m2m context while a
session closes, as upstream does (gen2 is not used on SM8350). Residual:
a fatal error that arrives after close checked for it, while vb2 frees the
client buffers, is not waited for.

ROG5: patch 0162, kernel k115. Not in upstream.
